<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khor Fakkan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Khawr Fakkan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khor Fakkan is a port town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-oman">Gulf of Oman</a> coast of the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a>, an exclave of the emirate of Sharjah. It was sacked by the Portuguese under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> in 1507 and later came under Qasimi rule. Its deep-water harbour, outside the Strait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>, is now one of the region's main container ports.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khor-fakkan.jpg" alt="Khor Fakkan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ashiq pang · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_best_Hiking_path_in_UAE_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Khor Fakkan, Arabic Khawr Fakkan, means '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a> of the Two Jaws', a name given for its position on a crescent-shaped bay flanked by two headlands. The city is an exclave of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Sharjah on the east coast of the United Arab Emirates, facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-oman">Gulf of Oman</a> and surrounded by the Emirate of Fujairah. After Fujairah City it is the second largest city on that coast. It lies between the Shumayliyah, or Western Hajar, mountains and the sea, with Jebel al Hilqah rising to 1,023 metres. The bay faces north-east and is protected from the prevailing winds by a jetty serving the container terminal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settlement is old. Post holes from the wooden uprights of barasti huts resemble those at Tell Abraq, which date from the 3rd to the 1st millennium BCE, and excavators from the Sharjah Archaeological Museum identified 34 graves and a settlement of the early to mid 2nd millennium BCE on rock outcrops above the harbour. Around 1500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duarte-barbosa">Duarte Barbosa</a> described a village with plentiful gardens and farms. </p><p>In the 16th century the Portuguese commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> captured it and called it Corfacão; it was one of a series of fortified places, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sohar">Sohar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hormuz">Hormuz</a>, by which the Portuguese controlled access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-oman">Gulf of Oman</a>. A walled belt closed the gorge to the interior, with a single door defended by a tower, to guard against tribal attacks.</p><p>The Portuguese fort was a ruin by 1666, when the log of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> vessel Meerkat described about 200 small houses of date branches, date palms, fig trees, melons and several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> of fresh water. An Omani invasion of 1623 under Sheikh Muhammad Suhari is one suggested reason for the ruin. In 1737 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> invaded with some 5,000 men and 1,580 horses, helped by the Dutch. In 1765 Carsten Niebuhr found Khor Fakkan belonged to a sheikh of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qasimi">Al Qasimi</a>, the ruling family of Sharjah. Around the turn of the 19th century, Lorimer notes, it had about 5,000 date trees and some 150 houses with about 800 people, who lived by cultivation and pearling.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Khor Fakkan Container Terminal, inaugurated in 1979, is the only natural deep-sea port in the region and one of the major container ports of the United Arab Emirates. Its project cost Dh 300 million, reclaimed about 150,000 square metres and built quays 16 metres deep for vessels over 400 metres long. The city also draws domestic tourists to its white sand beaches and coral reefs, and the German submarine U-533, sunk off the coast on 16 October 1943, was found by divers in 2009 at a depth of 108 metres.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khor Fakkan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sharjah also controlled the ports of <b>Khor Fakkan</b> and, for most of the period, Kalba on the Gulf of Oman coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sheikhdom-of-sharjah">Sheikhdom of Sharjah · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
